00:14:28,752 --> 00:14:29,653
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, why?

182
00:14:29,753 --> 00:14:39,515
[SPEAKER_01]: See, that's your assumption, that they would want to explain the choice, which means you want to take the control over the choice, which means it would want to put a center in your choice, which means it's taken as yourself.

183
00:14:40,055 --> 00:14:41,435
[SPEAKER_01]: It comes from the assumption of the self.

184
00:14:42,176 --> 00:14:43,616
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do you choose something over the other?

185
00:14:47,305 --> 00:14:55,491
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I mean, okay, I would... but what about, like, being... like, there's... there's tendencies in your mind.

186
00:14:55,511 --> 00:14:56,691
[SPEAKER_00]: There's underlying tendencies.

187
00:14:57,132 --> 00:14:57,292
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

188
00:14:57,312 --> 00:14:57,792
[SPEAKER_00]: In the mind.

189
00:14:57,852 --> 00:15:05,718
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you said... couldn't you say there was a reason for... Well, the thing is, see, it's the choice works in the same way.

190
00:15:05,738 --> 00:15:09,160
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, the other day, when we spoke, when I referred to that simile from the suttas of

191
00:15:10,255 --> 00:15:17,637
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, the rain falls on the top of the mountain, the little springs from the top of the mountain, but then, as they're coming down, they're getting bigger and bigger, because they're collecting more and more water.

192
00:15:18,137 --> 00:15:20,498
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you have the pools, mountain pools, filled with water.

193
00:15:20,518 --> 00:15:26,300
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, they start overflowing, they fill up the lower pools, and lower... and then, you have the ocean, but it all comes from the same water.

194
00:15:26,880 --> 00:15:29,120
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what you're asking now is already on the level of ocean.

195
00:15:29,140 --> 00:15:32,001
[SPEAKER_01]: It's already on the level of full development, and then you're, what did I choose?

196
00:15:32,621 --> 00:15:34,562
[SPEAKER_01]: But, basically, all of your choices that you're

197
00:15:37,232 --> 00:15:38,613
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, they're all formal directions.

198
00:15:38,793 --> 00:15:40,174
[SPEAKER_01]: Choice is a direction you choose.

199
00:15:41,914 --> 00:15:46,256
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, every particular direction has been determined by more general directions.

200
00:15:47,076 --> 00:15:49,557
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can say, well, why did I choose this right here?

201
00:15:50,457 --> 00:15:52,238
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do I choose this thing right here, right now?

202
00:15:53,058 --> 00:15:57,500
[SPEAKER_01]: And, there's always going to be an element of, kind of, spontaneity.

203
00:15:59,428 --> 00:15:59,948
[SPEAKER_01]: To a degree.

204
00:16:00,628 --> 00:16:03,049
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes more, sometimes less, depending how much people...

205
00:16:03,069 --> 00:16:08,450
[SPEAKER_01]: But spontaneity is a valid phenomenon, a circumstance, a chance.

206
00:16:09,270 --> 00:16:12,991
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a phenomenon that you can then choose to be the reason for your choice.

207
00:16:13,552 --> 00:16:18,933
[SPEAKER_01]: But more important than that, why you choose particular things is because of the stuff you chose in the past.

208
00:16:20,495 --> 00:16:38,484
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now people, for example, like, you know, there are many cases that I met of people who get inspired with Buddha's teaching, want to practice Buddha's teaching, but they've been out in the lay world for quite some time, accumulated things, accumulated people, accumulated family and children, and now they can't just choose to leave it all behind.

209
00:16:38,504 --> 00:16:38,984
[SPEAKER_01]: It's too much.

210
00:16:40,185 --> 00:16:44,547
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then they say, well, obviously, it's not my fault that I can't go and become a monk.

211
00:16:44,587 --> 00:16:45,468
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, actually, it is.

212
00:16:45,848 --> 00:16:47,389
[SPEAKER_01]: If you want to see it as a fault, it is a fault.

213
00:16:47,949 --> 00:16:48,129
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

214
00:16:48,469 --> 00:16:50,190
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of the 30 years beforehand,

215
00:16:50,580 --> 00:16:53,103
[SPEAKER_01]: You've been choosing all the things they've been taking away elsewhere.

216
00:16:54,145 --> 00:17:01,355
[SPEAKER_01]: And you did not consider at the time, when you wanted to find a wife and have children, that, who knows, in my 10 years I might despise it all.

217
00:17:01,995 --> 00:17:04,359
[SPEAKER_01]: In 15 years I might want to leave and become a monk.

218
00:17:04,379 --> 00:17:05,059
[SPEAKER_01]: You didn't think that.

219
00:17:05,700 --> 00:17:09,343
[SPEAKER_01]: You just thought what your sense desire was dictating you.

220
00:17:09,583 --> 00:17:11,985
[SPEAKER_01]: So often, people's choices are determined by senses.

221
00:17:12,405 --> 00:17:15,207
[SPEAKER_01]: Because senses are independent five animals.

222
00:17:15,428 --> 00:17:28,218
[SPEAKER_01]: They just drag you left and right, and then you think you... Well, you are choosing left or right in terms of the beautiful sights, because you've already given in to the pressure of beautiful sights, beautiful touches, agreeable tastings.

223
00:17:28,918 --> 00:17:31,520
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's most often why people choose.

224
00:17:31,840 --> 00:17:34,883
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they just take for granted the criteria of sense pleasure.

225
00:17:36,776 --> 00:17:41,559
[SPEAKER_01]: If you start contemplating that, the danger in sense... you stop choosing the sense pleasure.

226
00:17:41,939 --> 00:17:48,062
[SPEAKER_01]: When you stop choosing the sense pleasure, those pools will dry up, bit by bit by bit, and then they won't be fueled anymore.

227
00:17:48,082 --> 00:17:50,784
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, so there is the... there is the choice.

228
00:17:51,604 --> 00:17:54,206
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you want to find out, why did I choose to write this right now?

229
00:17:54,326 --> 00:17:55,287
[SPEAKER_01]: Why did I choose that?

230
00:17:55,747 --> 00:17:57,027
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, start looking back.

231
00:17:57,668 --> 00:17:59,389
[SPEAKER_01]: What did you choose beforehand and beforehand?

232
00:17:59,689 --> 00:18:00,630
[SPEAKER_01]: And, I mean, more general.

233
00:18:00,650 --> 00:18:04,292
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't mean in a linear sequence, what I've chosen five minutes ago and 15 minutes ago.

234
00:18:04,352 --> 00:18:04,612
