00:25:33,174 --> 00:25:42,059
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, you know, in the surface, there were people who just saw through it, who were, like, about to become kings or queens, with everything laid out for them, like, what everybody would just wish for.

331
00:25:42,439 --> 00:25:46,322
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, they were, like, disgusted with the whole thing and left it the next moment.

332
00:25:46,342 --> 00:25:48,143
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you say, why can't I leave it next moment?

333
00:25:48,163 --> 00:25:51,665
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, whenever you leave it, it's going to be left the next moment.

334
00:25:52,445 --> 00:25:53,686
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's how leaving works.

335
00:25:54,386 --> 00:25:59,449
[SPEAKER_01]: Why you can't reach to the next moment, where you leave things, is because you haven't been building it up.

336
00:26:00,149 --> 00:26:02,471
[SPEAKER_01]: And you build it up through simply attending to things.

337
00:26:03,155 --> 00:26:05,536
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yoniso manasikāra, the proper attention.

338
00:26:05,616 --> 00:26:07,237
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like the root of everything.

339
00:26:07,497 --> 00:26:08,518
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what you're saying.

340
00:26:08,678 --> 00:26:12,920
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can't know about those things you need to be attending to without... Oh, yeah.

341
00:26:13,040 --> 00:26:13,720
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

342
00:26:14,100 --> 00:26:15,561
[SPEAKER_00]: Without a good friend to help you.

343
00:26:15,581 --> 00:26:17,522
[SPEAKER_01]: So, without somebody telling you what you need to attend to.

344
00:26:18,122 --> 00:26:19,283
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I'm saying.

345
00:26:19,303 --> 00:26:22,645
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, that good friend is not in your control.

346
00:26:22,905 --> 00:26:25,906
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, it might be out of it, you know.

347
00:26:26,266 --> 00:26:26,647
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah.

348
00:26:26,667 --> 00:26:27,407
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I said.

349
00:26:27,427 --> 00:26:27,587
[SPEAKER_01]: Like,

350
00:26:30,975 --> 00:26:34,858
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a degree of just complete, like, circumstance.

351
00:26:35,699 --> 00:26:38,841
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, at the same time, as I said, circumstances...

352
00:26:38,861 --> 00:26:53,893
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, you might have been lucky to come across the Buddha's teaching, but luck has nothing to do with you choosing to practice or not, with you drilling and year after year making efforts of, like, undoing the ignorant habits of your mind.

353
00:26:54,983 --> 00:26:56,544
[SPEAKER_01]: There's absolutely no room for luck there.

354
00:26:57,324 --> 00:27:10,171
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why, yeah, sometimes in the sutras, the monks who have developed the Sāghi powers to see beyond, they will say, oh, yeah, I met that person because in the previous life we were close and then we were bound to kind of revolve around each other no matter where we were reborn and so on.

355
00:27:10,671 --> 00:27:12,272
[SPEAKER_01]: But all that is irrelevant.

356
00:27:13,032 --> 00:27:15,074
[SPEAKER_01]: How it came to be, it's irrelevant.

357
00:27:15,114 --> 00:27:15,334
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

358
00:27:15,354 --> 00:27:17,715
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're responsible for what you do on account of it.

359
00:27:18,678 --> 00:27:20,019
[SPEAKER_01]: You're responsible for what you choose.

360
00:27:20,639 --> 00:27:29,825
[SPEAKER_01]: And the thing is, see, many people, even back in the Buddhas, they came across the Buddha, but not all of them seen his wisdom, and not, like, not even half of them chose to follow it.

361
00:27:31,205 --> 00:27:37,109
[SPEAKER_01]: So, people can come across the purest of the dhamma instructions, and not even recognize it as such.

362
00:27:37,249 --> 00:27:37,529
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

363
00:27:37,549 --> 00:27:39,230
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they don't have that proper attention.

364
00:27:40,131 --> 00:27:43,352
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the two factors for the right view, proper attention in the absence of another.

365
00:27:43,913 --> 00:27:45,614
[SPEAKER_01]: And proper attention, basically,

366
00:27:46,467 --> 00:27:49,170
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a moment that you go, ah, I can't do it rightly the right second.

367
00:27:49,190 --> 00:27:49,311
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

368
00:27:49,331 --> 00:27:55,018
[SPEAKER_01]: Proper attention is an attitude of looking at things rightly, not obscuring the things.

369
00:27:56,187 --> 00:28:00,470
[SPEAKER_01]: So, a responsible authentic attitude will be an attitude of proper attention.

370
00:28:01,051 --> 00:28:07,956
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, if nothing is instructed to you to properly attend, that proper attention in itself cannot lead you out of the suffering.

371
00:28:08,556 --> 00:28:16,802
[SPEAKER_01]: But with the right attitude, the proper attitude, the attitude of proper attention, and the right instruction, you will know what to look for, you will know what to keep attending.

372
00:28:17,723 --> 00:28:21,145
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, as we spoke before, it can't... enlightenment can't happen by luck.

373
00:28:21,265 --> 00:28:24,447
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, attending to something rightly,

374
00:28:25,368 --> 00:28:27,770
[SPEAKER_01]: It has to be done way more than once.

375
00:28:28,651 --> 00:28:38,399
[SPEAKER_01]: It has to be done countless numbers of times until that handle of ignorance, of the axe of ignorance, wears thin and thinner and thinner and thinner.

376
00:28:38,980 --> 00:28:42,403
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you pick up the axe once, the wooden handle, there's absolutely no difference.

377
00:28:43,696 --> 00:28:52,101
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you don't know that you're supposed to keep doing it on and on and on and on and on and on for seven days, seven months, seven years, who knows, seven lifetimes.

378
00:28:52,801 --> 00:28:57,523
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't know that, there is nothing to indicate to you that you should pick that axe again.

379
00:28:58,684 --> 00:29:04,027
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, like, you can pick it once, twice, five times, by accident, by chance, by your own reasoning.

380
00:29:05,013 --> 00:29:10,959
[SPEAKER_01]: But there won't be any apparent proof or a verification that this is the way out of suffering.

381
00:29:11,459 --> 00:29:12,440
[SPEAKER_01]: Not anytime soon.

382
00:29:13,061 --> 00:29:17,425
[SPEAKER_01]: So, unless somebody tells you that that is the way of suffering and you keep sticking to it, you won't do it.

383
00:29:17,525 --> 00:29:19,226
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just like throwing a coin.

384
00:29:19,687 --> 00:29:29,676
[SPEAKER_01]: You can throw a coin and you can get head five times, ten times, but it's simply impossible that thousand times, thousand throws will always yield the same result.

385
00:29:29,796 --> 00:29:30,277
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

386
00:29:31,645 --> 00:29:32,365
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly it.

387
00:29:32,745 --> 00:29:39,447
[SPEAKER_01]: Doing this thousand times, without knowing that you're supposed to, it's not about head or tail, it's about actually throwing the coin, that's what undoes it.

388
00:29:40,547 --> 00:29:44,388
[SPEAKER_01]: Unless somebody tells you that, you won't know, because it won't be apparent.

389
00:29:44,908 --> 00:29:58,232
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why we, like, you know, some, somebody who did enlighten himself with his own efforts, without any external advice, is like the greatest, and through that simple trial and error, they took him, like, lifetimes to develop.

390
00:30:01,224 --> 00:30:23,842
[SPEAKER_01]: So, unless somebody tells you what you're doing now, keep doing it, maybe fine-tune it, or point out more, or whatever, inspiring similar ways to develop the same principle, unless you read it somewhere, unless you have faith in what you read, it's simply impossible that you'll repeat it exactly the same way for thousands of times, because there is no result to it.

391
00:30:24,957 --> 00:30:26,199
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no apparent result to it.

392
00:30:26,419 --> 00:30:28,781
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't see the axe handle wearing clean.

393
00:30:29,102 --> 00:30:29,903
[SPEAKER_01]: You just can't see it.

394
00:30:30,183 --> 00:30:31,685
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's the thing.

395
00:30:31,705 --> 00:30:42,377
[SPEAKER_00]: There might be someone who might want to ask, like, what can I have that will help me make the right choice all the time?

396
00:30:43,628 --> 00:30:43,848
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

397
00:30:44,289 --> 00:30:45,270
[SPEAKER_00]: What can I add?

398
00:30:45,330 --> 00:30:48,313
[SPEAKER_00]: What is necessary for making the right choice all the time?

399
00:30:48,493 --> 00:30:48,693
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

400
00:30:49,033 --> 00:30:50,234
[SPEAKER_01]: That's basically impossible.

401
00:30:50,815 --> 00:30:54,479
[SPEAKER_01]: You can know that you made the right choice only after you have repeated it.

402
00:30:54,579 --> 00:30:58,563
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, only after you discover you handled one thing, you'll know I wore a thing.

403
00:30:59,443 --> 00:31:00,284
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly it.

404
00:31:00,865 --> 00:31:03,788
[SPEAKER_01]: People think it's like throwing the coin and then you throw it and you got it right.

405
00:31:03,868 --> 00:31:04,028
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

406
00:31:05,028 --> 00:31:06,529
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter whether it's head or tail.

407
00:31:07,109 --> 00:31:12,653
[SPEAKER_01]: What matters is thousands and thousands and thousands of times of flicking and flicking and flicking.

408
00:31:12,853 --> 00:31:18,016
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you realize it wasn't about the head or tail, it was about wearing the dang coin down.

409
00:31:20,398 --> 00:31:24,560
[SPEAKER_01]: And wearing of the coin is irrelevant to the result that you get when you throw the coin.

410
00:31:24,640 --> 00:31:26,341
[SPEAKER_01]: It's your expectations that lead you to results.

411
00:31:27,062 --> 00:31:32,085
[SPEAKER_01]: But the actual effort of throwing it, regardless of the result, is what wore it down.

412
00:31:41,589 --> 00:31:45,612
[SPEAKER_00]: So, on another level, on another level of a difference.

413
00:31:46,132 --> 00:31:48,374
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, just a similar word further, just one more thing.

414
00:31:48,674 --> 00:32:07,947
[SPEAKER_01]: And, unless you know what you're supposed to do, through an affluence of another, be it reading, or suttas, or study, or whatever else, whatever way you might have arrived at it, it's impossible that you would have been repeating the same efforts in the same way

415
00:32:09,072 --> 00:32:09,732
[SPEAKER_01]: for a long time.

416
00:32:10,412 --> 00:32:11,293
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just impossible.

417
00:32:12,993 --> 00:32:15,454
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there is no guidance on what those efforts are.

418
00:32:15,674 --> 00:32:22,556
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're just using your own judgment, and then your own judgment is influenced by the inevitable expectation of pleasure in the form of peace or whatever.

419
00:32:22,976 --> 00:32:37,420
[SPEAKER_01]: But basically, you would be acting out of your pleasure, which means there would be some craving, which means, unless somebody tells you externally, and then you have faith into that more than in what your own, like, initial inclinations, eh... you will not do that.

420
00:32:38,479 --> 00:32:42,864
[SPEAKER_01]: Because then, you need to keep repeating that which is contrary to your own existence, going against the grain.

421
00:32:44,545 --> 00:32:49,931
[SPEAKER_01]: Since somebody... somebody defines the grain, so you can go against, you won't be able to go, because everything you do is with your grain.

422
00:32:51,553 --> 00:32:56,198
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need an external input in which you'll have faith to keep sticking to it.

423
00:32:58,817 --> 00:33:10,680
[SPEAKER_01]: But without that... without that external instruction, it's impossible that you would be repeating the same effort, the same quality of effort, sufficient amount of times.

424
00:33:11,080 --> 00:33:17,122
[SPEAKER_01]: So, a person can attend to the right view accidentally, and then they can do it again and again.

425
00:33:17,402 --> 00:33:25,964
[SPEAKER_01]: But if they never heard about the right view, if they never heard what the right view is, if they never heard what to develop right view, how to develop right view, if they never heard what to look for in the right view,

426
00:33:26,843 --> 00:33:29,726
[SPEAKER_01]: They'll never recognize that what they've just done was the right view.

427
00:33:30,547 --> 00:33:35,853
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means it's unthinkable that they'll repeat the same experience thousands and thousands and thousands of times.

428
00:33:36,274 --> 00:33:37,435
[SPEAKER_01]: It's simply unthinkable.

429
00:33:38,336 --> 00:33:38,516
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

430
00:33:38,556 --> 00:33:43,162
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they don't recognize that experience for what it is, so they won't know what to look for on and on and on.

431
00:33:43,839 --> 00:33:45,240
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they might accidentally do it.

432
00:33:45,260 --> 00:33:48,784
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's impossible to become even a sotāpanna by sheer accident.

433
00:33:49,304 --> 00:33:51,026
[SPEAKER_01]: Or by... Oh, I heard it once.

434
00:33:51,306 --> 00:33:57,632
[SPEAKER_01]: When people hear something once, like in the suttas, they think, yeah, they understood it once because their minds were already developed through these right habits beforehand.

435
00:33:58,332 --> 00:34:04,338
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the same repetition has been already developed and now they go like the last kind of drip that sort of covered it already.

436
00:34:05,079 --> 00:34:08,742
[SPEAKER_01]: But except that, you can't, you know, it comes down... it all comes down to the efforts.

437
00:34:12,178 --> 00:34:36,857
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if someone is riddled with lust at one point, at one time, riddled with anger, they want to know how can I simply not choose that direction, to go in that direction.

438
00:34:37,407 --> 00:34:38,688
[SPEAKER_00]: If I see that, I don't act.

439
00:34:38,708 --> 00:34:39,228
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a choice.

440
00:34:39,388 --> 00:34:44,530
[SPEAKER_00]: I can either continue with this lustful mode or not.

441
00:34:44,630 --> 00:34:46,831
[SPEAKER_00]: What stops me from...?

442
00:34:46,911 --> 00:34:51,912
[SPEAKER_00]: What makes me choose not to follow that lustful direction?

443
00:34:52,373 --> 00:34:54,834
[SPEAKER_00]: And what makes me choose to go follow the lustful?

444
00:34:55,074 --> 00:34:58,175
[SPEAKER_01]: See, if it comes to the point of being real, being right, but...

445
00:34:58,832 --> 00:35:05,515
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, what you need to recognize is that you kind of let yourself get into that state.

446
00:35:06,436 --> 00:35:11,038
[SPEAKER_01]: And once you're in that state, there is nothing else that you can do except endure.

447
00:35:12,699 --> 00:35:14,120
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is a form of choosing no.

448
00:35:15,801 --> 00:35:20,003
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why, you know, the strength of mind to just withstand no is necessary.

449
00:35:21,523 --> 00:35:23,424
[SPEAKER_01]: But that does not necessarily bring...

450
00:35:23,504 --> 00:35:28,387
[SPEAKER_01]: It's necessary when things kind of come up of such nature that you just have to endure them.

451
00:35:29,980 --> 00:35:33,881
[SPEAKER_01]: But, basically, that's only, like, one way of dealing with it at a time.

452
00:35:34,301 --> 00:35:38,862
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, fundamentally, you want to find a state where those things don't bother you at all, and there is no form of endurance.

453
00:35:39,282 --> 00:35:40,302
[SPEAKER_01]: And that comes with wisdom.

454
00:35:40,422 --> 00:35:42,603
[SPEAKER_01]: And that wisdom comes with foreseeing these things.

455
00:35:43,223 --> 00:35:46,844
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you ended up in a state of rag... rag by anger or by loss.

456
00:35:47,944 --> 00:35:51,685
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, you know, for days and weeks before, you didn't pay any attention to it.

457
00:35:52,165 --> 00:35:53,646
[SPEAKER_01]: Or you paid attention to it wrongly.

458
00:35:54,346 --> 00:35:59,027
[SPEAKER_01]: Or you were attending, or you were entertaining certain sensual notions, or certain irritable notions.

459
00:35:59,840 --> 00:36:07,886
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you know, paired with a few circumstances, with something that happened, with a bad mood, with this, with a lack of sleep, now results in this full-on anger.

460
00:36:09,092 --> 00:36:10,893
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, but what do I do right here, right now?

461
00:36:10,953 --> 00:36:12,714
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, what you do right here, right now, I recognize.

462
00:36:12,974 --> 00:36:17,956
[SPEAKER_01]: The right here, right now, was influenced by, you know, quite some time before, of your careless attending.

463
00:36:18,256 --> 00:36:22,518
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, you become even more concerned about attending things wrongly later on.

464
00:36:22,538 --> 00:36:23,538
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you actually learn from it.

465
00:36:24,039 --> 00:36:29,041
[SPEAKER_01]: But at the time in itself, just clench your teeth, press the thumb against the roof of your mouth and endure it.

466
00:36:29,461 --> 00:36:33,383
[SPEAKER_01]: As the Buddha said, sometimes, just you need to, like a stronger man, pinch down the weaker man.

467
00:36:34,596 --> 00:36:36,937
[SPEAKER_01]: But you have a reflective contemplation.

468
00:36:37,077 --> 00:36:37,937
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, of course.

469
00:36:37,957 --> 00:36:38,897
[SPEAKER_01]: This is my responsibility.

470
00:36:38,917 --> 00:36:40,698
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes, well, you just have to endure it.

471
00:36:40,838 --> 00:36:45,019
[SPEAKER_01]: Once you endure it, once you haven't given in, you recognize, yeah, this is how it came about.

472
00:36:45,219 --> 00:36:46,539
[SPEAKER_01]: It was through my careless attending.

473
00:36:46,599 --> 00:36:57,622
[SPEAKER_01]: So, so then you become more concerned about attending things carefully, which means such extreme examples where you just have to purely endure, will be arising less and less.

474
00:36:59,042 --> 00:36:59,202
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

475
00:36:59,222 --> 00:37:01,063
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're not... you're going away from it.

476
00:37:02,488 --> 00:37:07,970
[SPEAKER_01]: Like in the beginning, every thought of sensuality or anger is a wracking sensuality or wracking anger.

477
00:37:08,310 --> 00:37:11,292
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the mind is just left or right, everywhere and nowhere.

478
00:37:12,052 --> 00:37:26,058
[SPEAKER_01]: Once you start developing that mind, once you start recognizing this, the influence of the, you know, the mountain streams in filling down the oceans, then you start... you stop all the time to empty the oceans, you just go and deal with the mountain streams, because you know eventually these oceans will dry up.

479
00:37:27,658 --> 00:37:28,499
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in the same sense,

480
00:37:30,163 --> 00:37:38,805
[SPEAKER_01]: But carefully attending to things beforehand, such extreme experiences won't be waiting for you down the line.

481
00:37:39,745 --> 00:37:45,746
[SPEAKER_00]: But before you've done that... Before you've done that, you do need to endure a fair bit of it.

482
00:37:46,306 --> 00:37:49,587
[SPEAKER_01]: Depending how quickly you do it as well, how quickly you extract yourself from it.

483
00:37:49,727 --> 00:37:52,127
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to have that contemplation.

484
00:37:52,627 --> 00:37:56,468
[SPEAKER_01]: Very negatively, to some extent, it will come down to endurance every now and then.

485
00:37:57,308 --> 00:38:00,770
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is a valid aspect of practice, endurance.

486
00:38:00,790 --> 00:38:02,831
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's the thing.

487
00:38:02,931 --> 00:38:15,839
[SPEAKER_01]: No matter what circumstances have brought the options for your choice in front of you, it will be you choosing to... well, you intentionally intending some aspects of it, even at the time.

488
00:38:16,659 --> 00:38:21,782
[SPEAKER_01]: So, obviously, it can be very painful to intend the opposite, i.e.

489
00:38:21,802 --> 00:38:22,383
[SPEAKER_01]: to say no.

490
00:38:23,505 --> 00:38:29,171
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's only painful because your mind has been going in the direction of sensuality for such a long time.

491
00:38:29,191 --> 00:38:30,352
[SPEAKER_01]: You haven't been extracted from it.

492
00:38:30,372 --> 00:38:31,653
[SPEAKER_01]: You haven't been attending properly.

493
00:38:32,274 --> 00:38:38,400
[SPEAKER_01]: So now, when you have to say no, it's just so hard because the whole avalanche is already going and it's so painful to stop it.

494
00:38:38,600 --> 00:38:39,801
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, you can always do it.

495
00:38:39,821 --> 00:38:42,584
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's... That's what makes endurance easier.

496
00:38:43,264 --> 00:38:44,826
[SPEAKER_01]: You can always say no to anything.

497
00:38:45,426 --> 00:38:45,867
[SPEAKER_01]: Literally.

498
00:38:46,880 --> 00:38:48,200
[SPEAKER_01]: Literally.

499
00:38:48,840 --> 00:39:03,344
[SPEAKER_01]: If you have strength and wisdom in regard to... Like, you can have wisdom, you can know what you're supposed to choose, but if your mind is not developed, you will be swept by that avalanche of kind of predetermined directions that you've been cultivating for so long.

500
00:39:03,364 --> 00:39:04,344
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the wrong thing to do.

501
00:39:04,364 --> 00:39:06,945
[SPEAKER_01]: This is the wrong thing to do, but I can't help it, because I'm too weak.

502
00:39:08,165 --> 00:39:10,306
[SPEAKER_01]: But even then, you can say no to your weakness.

503
00:39:10,926 --> 00:39:12,426
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can always be a step ahead.

504
00:39:12,466 --> 00:39:13,567
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how reflection works.

505
00:39:13,587 --> 00:39:14,687
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how intention works.

506
00:39:16,269 --> 00:39:17,090
[SPEAKER_01]: Or step above.

507
00:39:17,490 --> 00:39:25,515
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can have a family and 15 kids, and absolutely nothing stops you, that the next moment you just walk out and never come back.

508
00:39:27,236 --> 00:39:32,940
[SPEAKER_01]: Or from your job, or from any other form of responsibility, that you yourself have accumulated.

509
00:39:33,861 --> 00:39:35,042
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is the accumulation.

510
00:39:35,162 --> 00:39:37,503
[SPEAKER_01]: The ocean is an accumulation of the water from the top.

511
00:39:39,044 --> 00:39:42,647
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you complain, but you are the one who left the tap open at the very top.

512
00:39:45,825 --> 00:39:46,826
[SPEAKER_01]: So, nothing stops you.

513
00:39:46,846 --> 00:39:48,647
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, at any given time, you just go and close it.

514
00:39:49,848 --> 00:39:51,350
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, just walk off.

515
00:39:52,190 --> 00:39:53,972
[SPEAKER_01]: But, what stops you is the weakness.

516
00:39:55,893 --> 00:40:03,760
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why, sometimes, people can do it over a period of time, when they have built up determination to leave, or determination to say no to whatever.

517
00:40:04,220 --> 00:40:06,983
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, sometimes, something extreme needs to happen.

518
00:40:08,207 --> 00:40:13,668
[SPEAKER_01]: Like in the suttas, when people met with extreme misfortune, unthinkable misfortunes, and that snapped them out of it and made them go the other way.

519
00:40:14,288 --> 00:40:19,049
[SPEAKER_01]: Or like the monks who haven't been quite fully developed in terms of arahantship, but they were there with the right view and everything.

520
00:40:19,329 --> 00:40:28,351
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, when they were about to die, when they were about to be set on fire by the by the highway robbers or whatever else, then they kind of abandoned it all internally.

521
00:40:28,371 --> 00:40:31,032
[SPEAKER_01]: When when thing got real.

522
00:40:33,312 --> 00:40:36,473
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, the person... they're responsible for investing

523
00:40:40,105 --> 00:40:46,670
[SPEAKER_00]: But nothing stops you to just walk out.

524
00:40:46,750 --> 00:40:47,911
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing stops you to walk.

525
00:40:47,951 --> 00:40:50,973
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing stops you, to the next moment, to refuse to breathe.

526
00:40:52,775 --> 00:40:55,677
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just, obviously, it's unpleasant to not breathe.

527
00:40:56,217 --> 00:40:57,378
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you never even consider it.

528
00:40:57,438 --> 00:40:58,939
[SPEAKER_01]: But breathing is another choice.

529
00:40:59,080 --> 00:40:59,700
[SPEAKER_01]: Neutral choice.

530
00:40:59,940 --> 00:41:00,881
[SPEAKER_01]: But something you keep doing.

531
00:41:01,161 --> 00:41:01,742
[SPEAKER_01]: It's an action.

532
00:41:03,883 --> 00:41:06,345
[SPEAKER_01]: So, nothing stops you to refuse to take a next in-breath.

533
