723
00:53:14,317 --> 00:53:18,562
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I want to run through the game where I will address that anger, so that I can never ever be angry in the future.

724
00:53:18,823 --> 00:53:19,884
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you see it, you'll respond.

725
00:53:19,904 --> 00:53:20,004
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

726
00:53:20,024 --> 00:53:21,786
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, basically, this is what bothers me.

727
00:53:21,906 --> 00:53:22,748
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll just shut it.

728
00:53:23,675 --> 00:53:24,536
[SPEAKER_01]: And how you shut it?

729
00:53:24,716 --> 00:53:26,757
[SPEAKER_01]: I shut it by going to a different monastery.

730
00:53:26,777 --> 00:53:29,119
[SPEAKER_01]: When that monastery starts bothering me, I go to another monastery.

731
00:53:29,339 --> 00:53:31,700
[SPEAKER_01]: Then I live on top of the hill where nobody will disturb me.

732
00:53:32,101 --> 00:53:37,865
[SPEAKER_01]: But I, myself, won't be doing anything about the possibility to be disturbed in the first place.

733
00:53:38,405 --> 00:53:39,225
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's a problem.

734
00:53:39,245 --> 00:53:42,207
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is not that you've been disturbed through anger or lust.

735
00:53:42,648 --> 00:53:44,409
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is that you lie about it.

736
00:53:45,339 --> 00:53:57,063
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if anger and lust never ever arise in the future, if you... if you're actually... if it remains as a possibility, it means you're still within the domain of anger and lust, which means there's no question... no... no idea, like, notion of freedom there.

737
00:53:57,363 --> 00:53:58,303
[SPEAKER_01]: Not the real one, at least.

738
00:53:59,043 --> 00:54:00,084
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what you recognize.

739
00:54:00,104 --> 00:54:02,164
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not about me suffering right here, right now.

740
00:54:02,184 --> 00:54:04,365
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about possibility to suffer in the future.

741
00:54:04,645 --> 00:54:07,486
[SPEAKER_01]: And that suffering in itself, that concern, is unpleasant.

742
00:54:08,507 --> 00:54:12,828
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you undo that, then you can't suffer in the present, by anything.

743
00:54:13,908 --> 00:54:21,571
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you cease to be liable to aversion, liable to lust, liable to delusion, even if you're in the middle of the village, nobody can make you angry.

744
00:54:22,191 --> 00:54:23,311
[SPEAKER_01]: Nobody can make you lustful.

745
00:54:25,872 --> 00:54:32,314
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, sometimes you need to extract yourself out from it, but you have to be clear as to why are you doing so.

746
00:54:32,834 --> 00:54:40,617
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether your desire is rooted in genuine desire to be free from suffering down the line, or if it's rooted in simple desire to run away.

747
00:54:42,487 --> 00:54:43,287
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, this bothers me.

748
00:54:43,427 --> 00:54:45,488
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll go when things are more pleasant and peaceful.

749
00:54:47,068 --> 00:54:53,189
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, you know, you won't be in control in the new situation, fundamentally.

750
00:54:53,269 --> 00:54:54,609
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when that changes...

751
00:54:54,649 --> 00:55:05,892
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why you have people who live, like, I don't know, I don't know, 20, 30 years, and if they ever end up back in the community or in a hospital with other people, they just lose it.

752
00:55:06,252 --> 00:55:06,932
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing.

753
00:55:07,332 --> 00:55:09,092
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no involvement, no development, nothing.

754
00:55:10,513 --> 00:55:11,913
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you have the other people,

755
00:55:12,883 --> 00:55:27,236
[SPEAKER_01]: You might have seen a degree of this, but then, they develop a view on account of it, which overrides, like, the current experience, because that's how... that's why views are dangerous, because it can contradict exactly what you're experiencing.

756
00:55:27,256 --> 00:55:35,943
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they might... they're developing a view that, no, you should never leave the group of people, because they will be only running away.

757
00:55:36,203 --> 00:55:36,844
[SPEAKER_01]: Not necessarily.

758
00:55:37,464 --> 00:55:40,027
[SPEAKER_01]: It can be running away, if that's what you act out of.

759
00:55:40,744 --> 00:55:42,244
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you act out of...

760
00:55:42,264 --> 00:55:48,266
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm gonna run away from this right now, so that I can deal with it fully, so that then I wouldn't care whether I'm alone or with others.

761
00:55:48,906 --> 00:55:50,127
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that's a good running away.

762
00:55:50,987 --> 00:55:57,088
[SPEAKER_01]: But people who kind of might have seen the danger in running away, or met a few people who ran away, then they came back and lost it.

763
00:55:58,249 --> 00:56:09,352
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, without trying it themselves, without distinguishing the... the... the... like the nature of the motive in itself, they end up developing the view that, no, leaving the group, leaving the... the... the setting is bad.

764
00:56:09,652 --> 00:56:09,912
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

765
00:56:10,632 --> 00:56:11,192
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no.

766
00:56:11,472 --> 00:56:14,874
[SPEAKER_01]: You make it good or bad depending on, like, the reasons for your action.

767
00:56:16,514 --> 00:56:18,915
[SPEAKER_01]: Depending whether it was rooted in wholesome or unwholesome.

768
00:56:20,176 --> 00:56:21,556
[SPEAKER_01]: And the view is unwholesome.

769
00:56:22,717 --> 00:56:22,957
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

770
00:56:23,017 --> 00:56:26,098
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you just override any good and bad by saying it's bad.

771
00:56:27,198 --> 00:56:30,199
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it can be good or can be bad depending on why you do it.

772
00:56:30,800 --> 00:56:31,700
[SPEAKER_01]: Like leaving the group.

773
00:56:32,297 --> 00:56:34,078
[SPEAKER_01]: Going to live alone, or similar.

774
00:56:34,218 --> 00:56:34,918
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you can't...

775
00:56:35,438 --> 00:56:39,300
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you put a blanket rule over it, then, okay, well, you just cover it, then that's bad.

776
00:56:39,600 --> 00:56:46,583
[SPEAKER_01]: Now you've covered it all up, and now you can't see the nature of it, which means there won't be any discernment of wholesome, un... rooted in wholesome, rooted in unwholesome.

777
00:56:47,483 --> 00:56:50,584
[SPEAKER_01]: You decided it beforehand.

778
00:56:52,425 --> 00:56:58,287
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I mean, both those things are... the externals... it's because of the external things.

779
00:56:58,307 --> 00:56:58,927
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

780
00:56:59,127 --> 00:57:00,008
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why I suffer.

781
00:57:00,088 --> 00:57:00,308
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

782
00:57:01,210 --> 00:57:08,852
[SPEAKER_01]: So, therefore, I'll stay in a community because... To keep the external things in the same way so I won't suffer.

783
00:57:09,012 --> 00:57:12,793
[SPEAKER_00]: Because being in it, there's many people that helps.

784
00:57:13,533 --> 00:57:24,095
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's not incidental that always those views that are kind of pasted over the actual particular things that you might be experiencing, those views are always fueled by the unwholesome.

785
00:57:25,895 --> 00:57:27,536
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you're not taking responsibility.

786
00:57:27,556 --> 00:57:28,397
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's it.

787
00:57:28,737 --> 00:57:31,459
[SPEAKER_01]: So, basically, I will condemn everybody that leaves the group.

788
00:57:31,740 --> 00:57:31,940
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

789
00:57:32,160 --> 00:57:34,702
[SPEAKER_01]: Because deep down inside, I'm terrified to do it myself.

790
00:57:35,983 --> 00:57:37,324
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, I will condemn the group.

791
00:57:37,845 --> 00:57:38,045
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

792
00:57:38,085 --> 00:57:41,848
[SPEAKER_01]: Because deep down inside, I'm incapable of living without people.

793
00:57:42,909 --> 00:57:44,210
[SPEAKER_01]: I just find it intolerable.

794
00:57:46,912 --> 00:57:53,397
[SPEAKER_01]: So, basically, people end up adopting their views based on the underlying kind of notions that are unwholesome.

795
00:57:54,359 --> 00:57:57,360
[SPEAKER_01]: Either fear, or hate, or lust, or whatever.

796
00:57:59,101 --> 00:58:08,305
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, like, oh, I will not leave, go and live on top of the mountain, I'll stay in the monastery, because the community is good, it's good to help people, it's good... And deep down inside, I'm simply afraid to go without food.

797
00:58:10,006 --> 00:58:11,226
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes it can be as simple as that.

798
00:58:11,906 --> 00:58:14,468
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm afraid that my food will be too rough on the mountain.

799
00:58:15,488 --> 00:58:19,730
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'll find every single justification to stay in the monastery.

800
00:58:23,243 --> 00:58:31,629
[SPEAKER_01]: Including meeting people, distractions, engagements, duties, sense of importance, sense of accomplishment, sense of courteousness, whatever else.

801
00:58:32,910 --> 00:58:39,655
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, I met quite a fair deal of other people who know being a monk is about living alone in the forest and doing this and that.

802
00:58:39,895 --> 00:58:40,155
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

803
00:58:40,875 --> 00:58:47,360
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they have such serious issues that they can't stand anywhere, no matter how kind and good they are to them.

804
00:58:48,529 --> 00:58:51,930
[SPEAKER_01]: They're just pure hatred towards another.

805
00:58:52,150 --> 00:59:08,135
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only way for me to remain in this kind of setting that I've sort of ended up in, is to run away from everybody and hide in the forest and just keep it to a bare minimum meeting with others, because I just can't stand them.

806
00:59:08,535 --> 00:59:09,656
[SPEAKER_01]: And I don't know how to deal with it.

807
00:59:09,696 --> 00:59:12,436
[SPEAKER_01]: I just lose my composure every single time.

808
00:59:13,657 --> 00:59:16,198
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, externally, you can paint either of those unwholesome.

809
00:59:16,218 --> 00:59:16,798
[SPEAKER_01]: That's wholesome.

810
00:59:17,493 --> 00:59:17,713
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

811
00:59:17,973 --> 00:59:19,374
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha said you live in the forest.

812
00:59:19,974 --> 00:59:23,597
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, he said something else in conjunction to that.

813
00:59:23,617 --> 00:59:26,318
[SPEAKER_01]: He said only those who are ready to live in the forest should live in the forest.

814
00:59:27,619 --> 00:59:29,320
[SPEAKER_01]: As the Buddha said you should keep in the community.

815
00:59:29,360 --> 00:59:34,123
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, yeah, he said only those who are kind of capable of keeping in the community should keep in the community.

816
00:59:34,883 --> 00:59:41,287
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he did not adopt a rule that kind of overrides everything else, regardless of the actual particular circumstances.

817
00:59:42,957 --> 00:59:46,080
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why he wouldn't have made all those dhūtangas mandatory.

818
00:59:46,300 --> 00:59:51,185
[SPEAKER_01]: And when Devadatta Nagarjuna has requested that, you know, rāyagraha should be mandatory, right?

819
00:59:51,245 --> 00:59:53,046
[SPEAKER_01]: If one thought should be mandatory.

820
00:59:53,547 --> 00:59:54,007
[SPEAKER_01]: So, no.

821
00:59:54,027 --> 00:59:55,989
[SPEAKER_01]: Let those who want to wear rāyagraha, wear rāyagraha.

822
00:59:56,029 --> 00:59:58,992
[SPEAKER_01]: Let those who don't want to do it, don't want to do it.

823
00:59:59,412 --> 01:00:05,098
[SPEAKER_01]: And whether they want to do it or not want to do it, should be determined, well, through their understanding of wholesome and unwholesome.

824
01:00:06,275 --> 01:00:08,819
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the person who understood wholesome is unwholesome, will want wholesome.

825
01:00:09,840 --> 01:00:17,010
[SPEAKER_01]: And being an ascetic is not necessarily wholesome for everyone, because people can do it for very wrong reasons.

826
01:00:17,030 --> 01:00:17,471
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

827
01:00:33,309 --> 01:00:44,794
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can refine that, exactly that thing, to the extent whereby you have inspiration to go out of the monastery and live in the mountains.

828
01:01:02,624 --> 01:01:03,685
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you recognize it.

829
01:01:05,126 --> 01:01:10,992
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not necessarily coming out of unwholesome, but it's coming out of, well, intoxication that came with inspiration.

830
01:01:12,193 --> 01:01:19,020
[SPEAKER_01]: And you realize, well, there might be a great deal of delusion involved in this, because I'm not acting out of genuine recognition that this would be good for me.

831
01:01:19,380 --> 01:01:22,303
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm acting because I'm so inspired and full of energy to do it.

832
01:01:23,444 --> 01:01:24,645
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you take it cautiously.

833
01:01:25,996 --> 01:01:36,443
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you go halfway up the mountain, so to speak, and see if that's what... if that's... if that's bringing wholesome to increase, unwholesome to decrease, or other way around.

834
01:01:36,743 --> 01:01:39,525
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's other way around, we'll go back, restart it another time.

835
01:01:40,686 --> 01:01:45,249
[SPEAKER_00]: But also, it... it clears up the question... when you have the question, what should I do?

836
01:01:47,090 --> 01:01:47,991
[SPEAKER_00]: Well...

837
01:01:48,011 --> 01:01:48,351
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

838
01:01:49,251 --> 01:01:49,912
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't do those.

839
01:01:49,932 --> 01:01:50,813
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I'm intending.

840
01:01:50,833 --> 01:01:51,893
[SPEAKER_00]: I want to be free from suffering.

841
01:01:51,913 --> 01:01:52,674
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whatever I do,

842
01:01:53,570 --> 01:02:01,351
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not bad to have sexual intercourse.

843
01:02:01,431 --> 01:02:03,232
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not bad to have a wife.

844
01:02:03,332 --> 01:02:04,672
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not bad to have kids.

845
01:02:05,032 --> 01:02:05,552
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's not.

846
01:02:06,392 --> 01:02:07,353
[SPEAKER_00]: It depends on what you want to do.

847
01:02:07,553 --> 01:02:09,713
[SPEAKER_00]: It depends on what you want to do.

848
01:02:11,913 --> 01:02:15,914
[SPEAKER_01]: People think in terms of the eternal judgment.

849
01:02:16,054 --> 01:02:17,474
[SPEAKER_01]: This is bad, this is good.

850
01:02:17,954 --> 01:02:18,915
[SPEAKER_01]: It depends on what you want to do.

851
01:02:19,715 --> 01:02:20,635
[SPEAKER_01]: Killing is always bad.

852
01:02:21,940 --> 01:02:24,941
[SPEAKER_01]: Lying is... breaking five precepts is always bad.

853
01:02:25,501 --> 01:02:30,403
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's the five... it's the basic requirement that distinguishes humans from animals, if they stick to it.

854
01:02:31,524 --> 01:02:32,384
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there are those things.

855
01:02:32,604 --> 01:02:35,285
[SPEAKER_01]: But everything else, further on, well, that's not...

856
01:02:35,366 --> 01:02:38,227
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not judging people who have families and don't want to leave it.

857
01:02:39,087 --> 01:02:42,128
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm just saying that if this is what you want to do, well, that contradicts it.

858
01:02:42,649 --> 01:02:43,869
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't have it both ways.

859
01:02:43,969 --> 01:02:50,612
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't be saying, I'm a genuine practitioner of the Buddha's teaching and at the same time be engaging in sexual intercourse, whatever.

860
01:02:50,632 --> 01:02:51,092
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you're not.

861
01:02:51,796 --> 01:02:55,939
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the Buddha said himself, you can't have it open.

862
01:02:55,979 --> 01:02:57,060
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's exactly the thing.

863
01:02:57,160 --> 01:02:57,560
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not...

864
01:02:58,601 --> 01:03:14,293
[SPEAKER_01]: It's more like, you recognize, for yourself, whether the things you want to do, your choices, your intentions, even the way you attend to things, are being rooted in lust, aversion and delusion, or not.

865
01:03:16,268 --> 01:03:20,251
[SPEAKER_01]: But something was... Externally, you... that's irrelevant.

866
01:03:20,851 --> 01:03:24,474
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, there is a basis of the five precepts or the eight precepts later on.

867
01:03:25,294 --> 01:03:30,138
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, everything else in between can be, you know, very good and very bad externally.

868
01:03:30,658 --> 01:03:32,980
[SPEAKER_01]: But internally, actually, you are the only one they would know.

869
01:03:35,982 --> 01:03:36,302
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, no.

870
01:03:36,622 --> 01:03:37,082
[SPEAKER_01]: It's good.

871
01:03:37,563 --> 01:03:38,383
[SPEAKER_01]: It's good to go...

872
01:03:39,264 --> 01:03:42,206
[SPEAKER_01]: It's good to sit on a group retreat or something.

873
01:03:42,987 --> 01:03:43,567
[SPEAKER_01]: Unnecessary.

874
01:03:45,006 --> 01:03:49,708
[SPEAKER_00]: But someone says like, yeah, I want to be...

875
01:03:49,768 --> 01:03:55,071
